const e=[{id:"sv-2-1241",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book's introduction to MSA, what does a Gage R&R study need as a minimum?",options:["One individual measuring one part once with each of several gauges","At least two individuals measuring several parts at random on a few trials","A single reference part measured once a day for a month","Several parts measured once each and compared with the specification"],answer:1,hint:"Repeatability needs repeats; reproducibility needs more than one person.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 173: a Gage R&R evaluates consistency 'after having **at least two individuals** measure **several parts at random** on a **few trials**'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Repeated trials give repeatability; several individuals give reproducibility."}],final:"≥ 2 individuals, several parts, a few trials → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — one reading per gauge gives no repeated trials and no second appraiser.","","Incorrect — one reference part over time is a stability study.","Incorrect — single readings against the specification are a capability or inspection check."],bookRef:"Book p. 173"},{id:"sv-2-1242",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"In the book's MSA model, what can the **item to be measured** be?",options:["Only a physical part with a critical dimension","Only an output read by a computerised gauge","A physical part, a document or a scenario for customer service","Only a characteristic that has two-sided specification limits"],answer:2,hint:"The book wants MSA to apply to transactional projects as well as manufacturing.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 173: the item measured can be **a physical part, a document or a scenario for customer service**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"This is why MSA is applicable to about 98% of projects, not only machining ones."}],final:"Item = part, document or service scenario → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book explicitly includes documents and service scenarios, so MSA also applies to transactional work.","Incorrect — manual and judgement-based measurements are measurement systems too.","","Incorrect — MSA does not depend on the type of specification; one-sided or none still needs a trusted measurement."],bookRef:"Book pp. 172–182"},{id:"sv-2-1243",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"What does partitioning the sources of variation in an MSA allow the team to do, according to the book?",options:["Prove which process input causes the defects in the output","Show whether the process output meets the specification limits","Confirm that the process mean has not shifted over time","Plan action on the biggest sources of measurement error"],answer:3,hint:"MSA studies the measurement system itself, then targets its largest error source.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 174: MSA mathematically partitions variation **within the measurement system** so an **action plan** can attack the biggest contributors of measurement error."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The other options describe Analyze, capability and process stability — all about the process, not the gauge."}],final:"Partition → action plan on biggest measurement-error source → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — proving root-cause X's is the job of Analyze hypothesis tests, not MSA.","Incorrect — comparing output with specifications is process capability (2.4.1).","Incorrect — watching the process mean over time is process stability and control charting.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 174"},{id:"sv-2-1244",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book, what is the only way to know how good your data are?",options:["Check that the gauge has a calibration sticker","Run a process capability study","Plot a histogram of the collected data","Perform a Measurement System Analysis"],answer:3,hint:"Before trusting data, the book names one study that tells you how good it is.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 175: when solving a problem 'we must know how good our data are and the only way to do this is to perform a **Measurement System Analysis**'."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Calibration, capability and histograms all assume the data are already sound."}],final:"Only an MSA tells you how good the data are → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — calibration addresses bias only; MSA covers the entire system, not just calibration.","Incorrect — capability uses the data; it cannot tell whether the data themselves are trustworthy.","Incorrect — a histogram shows the data's shape, not how much of it is measurement error.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 175"},{id:"sv-2-1245",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:'The book asks: *"Would you use a football field to measure the gap in a spark plug?"* Which measurement-system property does this illustrate?',options:["Bias","Linearity","Resolution","Stability"],answer:2,hint:"Think about whether the scale goes down far enough to see any variation.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 179: identical readings can mean the gauge 'does not have the proper **resolution**' — the scale does not go down far enough."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A football-field scale cannot show the tiny variation in a spark-plug gap."}],final:"Scale too coarse = resolution → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — bias is an offset of the average from the true value, not a scale that is too coarse.","Incorrect — linearity is a change in bias across the operating range.","","Incorrect — stability is a change in bias over time."],bookRef:"Book p. 179"},{id:"sv-2-1246",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"In the book's experience of MSA projects on critical measurement points, how often do the measurements fail in one way or another?",options:["About 5%","About 20%","About 50%","More than 80%"],answer:3,hint:"The book stresses how common it is to discover poor measurement systems.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 177: 'It is not uncommon for **more than 80%** of the measurements to fail in one way or another'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"That is why the book insists on MSA before trusting any data."}],final:"More than 80% fail in some way → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — far too low; the book says failure is the norm, not the exception.","Incorrect — too low; the book reports that most critical measurements fail in some way.","Incorrect — still too low; the book's figure is a large majority.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 177"},{id:"sv-2-1247",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:'The book warns: *"On a cross country trip do not assume your gasoline gage is gospel."* Which point is it making?',options:["A gauge is accurate as long as it is new","A gauge's readings should be checked against a reference","A gauge only needs checking when it shows a sudden jump","A gauge with a digital display cannot be biased"],answer:1,hint:"The warning sits on the book's accuracy page.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 181: a system that consistently over- or under-estimates is **inaccurate**; accuracy is assessed against a known standard, another method or a theoretical value."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"So never take a gauge's reading on trust — check it."}],final:"Do not trust a gauge without checking accuracy → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — new gauges also need qualifying; the book lists MSA for new equipment.","","Incorrect — a gauge can be consistently wrong without ever showing a jump.","Incorrect — any instrument, digital or not, can over- or under-estimate."],bookRef:"Book p. 181"},{id:"sv-2-1248",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book, what happens if a measurement system does not generate accurate or precise enough data?",options:["The process automatically becomes unstable","The specification limits must be tightened","Decisions are made that generate errors, waste and cost","The customer's requirements change"],answer:2,hint:"Think about the decisions made from poor data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 175: 'If the measurement system does not generate accurate or precise enough data we will make the decisions that generate **errors, waste and cost**'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"This is why MSA comes before analysis."}],final:"Poor data → wrong decisions, waste, cost → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a poor gauge distorts what we see; it does not change the process itself.","Incorrect — specifications come from the customer and do not change to suit the data.","","Incorrect — customer requirements are independent of how we measure."],bookRef:"Book p. 175"},{id:"sv-2-1249",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"According to the book, which qualities make a measure **appropriate**?",options:["Specific, measurable, achievable and relevant","Accurate, precise, stable and linear","Sufficient, relevant, representative and contextual","Sufficient, reliable, rapid and certified"],answer:2,hint:"Four words: available often enough, useful, covers shifts and people, comes with context.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 177: appropriate measures are **sufficient** (available regularly), **relevant** (help isolate the problem), **representative** (across shifts and people) and **contextual** (collected with explanatory information)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"SMART belongs to goal statements; accuracy terms belong to MSA results."}],final:"Sufficient, relevant, representative, contextual → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — these are SMART criteria for objectives, not the book's qualities of a measure.","Incorrect — these are properties of a gauge examined in MSA, not the book's list for appropriate measures.","","Incorrect — only 'sufficient' is on the book's list; the other three are invented."],bookRef:"Book p. 177"},{id:"sv-2-1250",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In the book's time-estimate exercise, partners each estimate a 10-second interval six times while the other times it with a watch. What does the exercise show?",options:["Watches are less accurate than human estimates","A process becomes capable once it is timed","Estimates are unbiased if they are repeated six times","People used as instruments vary within and between themselves"],answer:3,hint:"Think of each person as a measurement system.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 180: the exercise demonstrates 'how well you can estimate a 10 second time interval' — each person is the instrument."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The six estimates vary for one person (repeatability) and between partners (reproducibility)."}],final:"People vary within and between themselves → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the watch is the reference; the estimates are what vary.","Incorrect — capability compares a process with specifications; the exercise is about measurement variation.","Incorrect — repeating an estimate shows its spread; it does not remove any bias.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 180"},{id:"sv-2-1251",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"The book says measurement variation always adds to the true variation, with one exception. What is the exception?",options:["When the gauge has been calibrated within the last month","When discrimination is so poor that every item reads the same","When the process being measured is in statistical control","When the measurements are taken by a computerised system"],answer:1,hint:"Think of a gauge so coarse that every reading is identical.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 176: measurement variation is additive to true variation — 'the only exception is when the **discrimination** of the measurement system is so poor it virtually sees everything the same'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A too-coarse scale hides variation instead of adding to it."}],final:"Exception = discrimination so poor everything looks the same → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — calibration addresses bias; it does not stop measurement variation adding to true variation.","","Incorrect — process control describes the process, not whether measurement error adds to it.","Incorrect — computerised systems tend to measure better, but they still add some variation."],bookRef:"Book p. 176"},{id:"sv-2-1252",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:`A team's process spread looks wide. The book warns that you *"may actually be producing a better product or service than you think"*. Why?`,options:["Measurement variation cancels part of the true variation, so the spread is understated","Measurement variation adds to the true variation, so the observed spread overstates it","Measurement variation moves the specification limits closer together","Measurement variation changes the process mean permanently"],answer:1,hint:"Observed variation = true variation + measurement variation.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 176: measurement variation is **additive** to the true variation."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"So a wide observed spread may be partly the gauge — the real product can be better than the data suggest (if the system is also accurate)."}],final:"Additive measurement error inflates observed spread → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the components are additive; measurement error does not cancel true variation.","","Incorrect — specification limits come from the customer and are not altered by the gauge.","Incorrect — measurement error affects the readings, not the real process mean."],bookRef:"Book p. 176"},{id:"sv-2-1253",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:'The book opens its MSA module by recalling what it calls the **"heart and soul"** of Six Sigma. What is it?',options:["Customers","Data","Statistics","Teamwork"],answer:1,hint:"MSA exists because everything else depends on this.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 172: 'We have learned the heart and soul of Six Sigma is **data**' — so we must know whether it is accurate and precise."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"That is why MSA comes before any analysis."}],final:"Heart and soul = data → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — customers define requirements, but the book's MSA opening line names data.","","Incorrect — statistics are tools for analysing data; the book names the data itself.","Incorrect — teamwork matters, but it is not the book's 'heart and soul' phrase here."],bookRef:"Book p. 172"},{id:"sv-2-1254",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book, which measurements **tend to be the best** in quality?",options:["Measurements estimated by experienced staff","Measurements recorded by hand on paper","Measurements taken by computer","Measurements made by the customer"],answer:2,hint:"The book comments on how measurement quality varies with the type of system.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 174: the quality of measurements varies significantly — 'with those **taken by computer** tending to be the best'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Even so, a computerised system still needs an MSA."}],final:"Computer measurements tend to be best → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book warns that some judgement measures are so poor you might as well guess.","Incorrect — manual recording adds transcription error; it is not singled out as the best.","","Incorrect — customer and supplier data often disagree with ours, which the book says signals a measurement problem."],bookRef:"Book p. 174"},{id:"sv-2-1255",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In the book's MSA model, what is the **reference**?",options:["It is the method used to perform the test","It is the surroundings where the measures are performed","It is the device used to measure the product","It is a standard used to calibrate the equipment"],answer:3,hint:"Each box in the MSA model has a one-line definition in the book.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 173: 'Reference is a **standard that is used to calibrate** the equipment'."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Procedure = method; environment = surroundings; equipment = device."}],final:"Reference = calibration standard → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the method used to perform the test is the procedure.","Incorrect — the surroundings are the environment.","Incorrect — the measuring device is the equipment.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 173"},{id:"sv-2-1256",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about precise data is CORRECT, according to the book?",options:["Precise data are independent of who measures them or when","Precise data always have an average equal to the reference value","Precise data can only come from automatic gauges","Precise data require a linearity study before collection"],answer:0,hint:"Precision combines repeatability (when) and reproducibility (who).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 179: 'Precise data are **independent of who measures them or when** the measurement is made'."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Average-on-target is accuracy; precision is about small spread only."}],final:"Precise = same result regardless of who or when → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — an average on the reference value is accuracy; data can be precise yet biased.","Incorrect — manual measurements can also be precise if repeatability and reproducibility are small.","Incorrect — linearity is an accuracy property across the range, not a condition for precision."],bookRef:"Book p. 179"},{id:"sv-2-1257",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the purpose of measurement is CORRECT, according to the book?",options:["More data-collection systems always lead to better decisions","Measurements must provide information and, ultimately, knowledge","Metrics should be chosen before deciding what you need to know","Computerised measurements never need to be checked with an MSA"],answer:1,hint:"Recall the book's 'Measurement Purpose' page.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 174: 'In order to be worth collecting measurements must provide value — that is, they must provide us with **information and, ultimately, knowledge**'."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Building data systems first, or skipping MSA for computers, contradicts the book."}],final:"Measurements must give information and knowledge → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book warns against complex data systems built without knowing how the data benefit the organisation.","","Incorrect — the book says 'What do I need to know?' must be answered first.","Incorrect — computer measurements tend to be best, but every measurement system still has error."],bookRef:"Book p. 174"},{id:"sv-2-1258",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about measurement **resolution** is CORRECT?",options:["Coarser resolution reduces the bias of the gauge","Coarser resolution improves the repeatability of the gauge","Data piled on a few values can understate the true variation","Resolution matters only in attribute measurement systems"],answer:2,hint:"A coarse scale hides small differences between readings.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Resolution is the smallest change the system can detect; the book flags identical readings as a sign the scale 'does not go down far enough' (p. 179)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"When readings pile up on a few values the true variation is hidden, so it is understated."}],final:"Coarse resolution hides variation → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — resolution is how finely the gauge reads; it does not change the offset from the true value.","Incorrect — identical readings from a coarse gauge are a warning flag, not real repeatability.","","Incorrect — resolution is a concern for variable (continuous) gauges in particular."],bookRef:"Book p. 179"},{id:"sv-2-1259",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the **operator** in the book's MSA model is CORRECT?",options:["It is always the most experienced inspector on the line","It is the standard used to calibrate the equipment","It is the documented method used to perform the test","It can be a person or an instrument measuring the products"],answer:3,hint:"The book widens 'operator' beyond people.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 173: 'Operator can refer to a person or can be **different instruments** measuring the same products'."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Reference = calibration standard; procedure = test method."}],final:"Operator = person or instrument → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — any appraiser who measures is an operator; experience is not part of the definition.","Incorrect — the standard used to calibrate is the reference.","Incorrect — the documented method is the procedure.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 173"},{id:"sv-2-1260",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements describe uses of MSA listed in the book?

1. Compare internal inspection standards with the standards of the customer.
2. Qualify new inspection equipment.
3. Determine whether the process output meets the specification limits.
4. Prove which process input causes the defects.

Which statements are CORRECT?`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:0,hint:"MSA uses are about measurement standards and equipment, not process performance.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 176: MSA can compare internal inspection standards with the customer's, highlight calibration-training needs, evaluate inspector training, compare existing equipment and **qualify new equipment**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Statements 3 (capability) and 4 (Analyze) are about the process, so the correct pair is 1 and 2."}],final:"Uses 1 and 2 are MSA uses → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — includes 3, which describes process capability, not a use of MSA.","Incorrect — 3 is capability and 4 is Analyze root-cause testing; neither is an MSA use.","Incorrect — includes 4, which is proving X's with hypothesis tests in Analyze."],bookRef:"Book p. 176"},{id:"sv-2-1261",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"statements",difficulty:3,stem:`Consider these statements about resolution.

1. Identical readings on repeated measurements always prove an excellent gauge.
2. Resolution is the smallest change a measurement system can detect.
3. A scale that is too coarse can hide real variation.
4. Calibrating a gauge improves its resolution.

Which statements are CORRECT?`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:1,hint:"Resolution is how finely the scale reads; calibration is about offset.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 179: if readings are always exactly the same, 'this is a flag' — the gauge may lack proper resolution."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"So 2 (definition) and 3 (coarse scale hides variation) are true; 1 and 4 are false."}],final:"Statements 2 and 3 are true → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — includes 1; identical readings are a warning flag of poor resolution, not proof of quality.","","Incorrect — includes 4; calibration corrects bias, it does not make the scale finer.","Incorrect — 1 and 4 are both false: identical readings are a flag and calibration does not change resolution."],bookRef:"Book p. 179"},{id:"sv-2-1262",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

Measurement System Analysis is the entire system, NOT just [?] or how good the measurement instrument is.`,options:["calibration","capability","sampling","stability"],answer:0,hint:"The book warns against treating MSA as one familiar maintenance activity.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 173: MSA 'is the entire system NOT just **calibration** or how good the measurement instrument is'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The full system includes the item, operator, equipment, procedure, reference and environment."}],final:"Not just calibration → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — capability describes the process against specifications, not the measurement system.","Incorrect — sampling is how data are selected; the book's sentence contrasts MSA with calibration.","Incorrect — stability is one component of accuracy inside MSA, not the narrow view the book warns against."],bookRef:"Book p. 173"},{id:"sv-2-1263",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

MSA is a [?] procedure to quantify the variation introduced to a process or product by the act of measuring.`,options:["graphical","mathematical","qualitative","visual"],answer:1,hint:"MSA puts numbers on measurement variation.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 173: 'MSA is a **mathematical** procedure to quantify variation introduced to a process or product by the act of measuring'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"It lets the team evaluate the measurement environment mathematically."}],final:"MSA = mathematical procedure → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — graphs help interpret an MSA, but the book calls MSA itself a mathematical procedure.","","Incorrect — MSA quantifies variation; a qualitative procedure would not put a number on it.","Incorrect — visual checks cannot partition variation into its sources."],bookRef:"Book p. 173"},{id:"sv-2-1264",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:2,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

A measure is [?] when it can detect variation across shifts and people.`,options:["contextual","relevant","representative","sufficient"],answer:2,hint:"Which quality of an appropriate measure is about covering the whole process?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 177: **representative** measures 'can detect variation across shifts and people'."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The other three words are the remaining qualities of an appropriate measure."}],final:"Across shifts and people = representative → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — contextual means collected with other information that might explain variation.","Incorrect — relevant means it helps understand and isolate the problem.","","Incorrect — sufficient means it is available to be measured regularly."],bookRef:"Book p. 177"},{id:"sv-2-1265",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Poor measures can result from all of the following EXCEPT:",options:["Collecting the data across all shifts and agents","Difficult measures","Poor sampling","Lack of understanding of the definitions"],answer:0,hint:"One option describes a quality of a good measure.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 177: poor measures result from poor operational definitions, difficult measures, poor sampling, lack of understanding of definitions and inaccurate or non-calibrated devices."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Collecting across shifts and agents makes a measure representative — a good quality, not a cause of poor measures."}],final:"Covering all shifts is good practice → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — difficult measures are on the book's list of causes of poor measures.","Incorrect — poor sampling is on the book's list of causes of poor measures.","Incorrect — lack of understanding of the definitions is on the book's list."],bookRef:"Book p. 177"},{id:"sv-2-1266",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which is NOT a reason the book gives for why MSA is important?",options:["To compare measurements between operators","To set the customer's specification limits","To evaluate a gauge before and after repair","To determine true process variation"],answer:1,hint:"MSA judges measurements; who decides the specification?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 176 'Why MSA?': study % variation from measurement, compare operators, compare devices, accept new systems, evaluate a suspect gauge, before/after repair, true process variation, training effectiveness."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Specification limits come from the customer (VOC), not from MSA."}],final:"Setting specifications is not an MSA purpose → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — comparing operators is on the book's list of reasons for MSA.","","Incorrect — evaluating a gauge before and after repair is on the book's list.","Incorrect — determining true process variation is on the book's list."],bookRef:"Book p. 176"},{id:"sv-2-1267",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"In the book's list of examples of what and when to measure (and check with MSA), which is NOT included?",options:["Decision points in process maps","Before and after process changes","Only the final output, never the X's","Prior to planning designed experiments"],answer:2,hint:"The book's list covers Y's, X's, gauges and key moments in the project.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 178: measure primary and secondary metrics, decision points, all gauges, **X's in the process**, before hypothesis testing, modelling and DOE, before/after changes, and to qualify operators."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Excluding the X's contradicts the list, so it is the odd one out."}],final:"X's must be measured too → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — decision points in process maps are on the book's list.","Incorrect — before and after process changes is on the book's list.","","Incorrect — prior to planning designed experiments is on the book's list."],bookRef:"Book p. 178"},{id:"sv-2-1268",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"According to the book, measurement error compromises decisions affecting several groups. Which group is NOT on that list?",options:["Customers","Producers","Regulators","Suppliers"],answer:2,hint:"The book names the three parties in a supply chain.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 177: 'Measurement Error compromises decisions affecting: **Customers, Producers, Suppliers**'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Regulators are not on the book's list."}],final:"List = customers, producers, suppliers → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — customers are on the book's list of those affected by measurement error.","Incorrect — producers are on the book's list of those affected by measurement error.","","Incorrect — suppliers are on the book's list of those affected by measurement error."],bookRef:"Book p. 177"},{id:"sv-2-1269",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A shaft diameter must be held within **±0.05 mm**. Operators measure it with a steel rule marked in **whole millimetres**. Which measurement-system problem is MOST evident?",options:["Poor reproducibility","Poor linearity","Poor stability","Inadequate resolution"],answer:3,hint:"Compare the smallest scale step with the size of variation that matters.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Resolution is the smallest change a gauge can show; the book's 'football field for a spark-plug gap' warning applies."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A 1 mm scale cannot see differences within a ±0.05 mm tolerance."}],final:"Scale far coarser than tolerance = resolution → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — reproducibility is a difference between appraisers' averages; nothing here involves several appraisers.","Incorrect — linearity is a change in bias across the range; the issue is scale fineness.","Incorrect — stability is drift over time; no time pattern is described.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 179"},{id:"sv-2-1270",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A logistics depot has **two floor scales**. The team wants to know whether both give the same results before pooling their weights in an analysis. Which approach fits this need?",options:["An MSA comparing the two measurement devices","A capability study of parcel weights","A control chart of daily parcel volumes","An FMEA of the weighing process"],answer:0,hint:"One of the book's reasons for MSA is to compare devices.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 176: MSA is used to **compare measurements between two (or more) measurement devices**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Capability, control charts and FMEA all study the process, not the scales."}],final:"Comparing devices = MSA → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — capability judges the process against specifications and assumes the scales are already trusted.","Incorrect — a control chart of volumes monitors the process, not agreement between devices.","Incorrect — an FMEA ranks risks; it does not test whether two gauges agree."],bookRef:"Book p. 176"},{id:"sv-2-1271",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A network team extracts its fault-repair KPI from a legacy system **only once per quarter**, because the extract takes days. The Green Belt needs weekly baseline data. Which quality of an appropriate measure is missing?",options:["Contextual","Relevant","Representative","Sufficient"],answer:3,hint:"Which quality is about being available to be measured regularly?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 177: **sufficient** = available to be measured regularly; otherwise it takes too long to gather data."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A quarterly-only extract cannot give weekly data, so the measure is not sufficient."}],final:"Not available regularly = not sufficient → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — contextual means collected with other explanatory information; frequency is the issue here.","Incorrect — the KPI may well help isolate the problem; the issue is how often it can be collected.","Incorrect — representative is about covering shifts and people, not how often data are available.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 177"},{id:"sv-2-1272",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:'A new digital micrometer displays readings to **0.001 mm**. No MSA has been done. The supervisor says: *"It reads to three decimal places, so it must be precise."* Which conclusion is justified?',options:["The micrometer is precise but may be inaccurate, so only a bias study is needed","Fine resolution does not prove precision; R&R must still be studied","The micrometer is precise because resolution and precision mean the same thing","The micrometer needs no MSA because digital gauges have no operator effect"],answer:1,hint:"Separate how finely a gauge reads from how much its repeated readings vary.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Resolution = smallest readable step; precision = spread of repeated measurements (repeatability + reproducibility)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Adequate resolution is necessary but not sufficient — the R&R must still be measured."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The other options assume precision without evidence or deny operator effects."}],final:"Resolution ≠ precision; study R&R → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — precision has not been shown; a fine display does not tell us the spread of repeated readings.","","Incorrect — resolution is how finely it reads; precision is the spread of repeat readings.","Incorrect — different appraisers can still position the part or apply force differently (reproducibility)."],bookRef:"Book pp. 174, 179",trap:"Many decimal places look impressive but say nothing about repeatability."},{id:"sv-2-1273",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:`Loan officers grade applications as low, medium or high risk **by judgement**; no instrument is used. A colleague says: *"There's no gauge, so MSA doesn't apply."* Which response is BEST supported by the book?`,options:["MSA applies only to physical parts, so the colleague is right","MSA applies only once the officers are given a scoring tool","The officers are the instrument, so MSA can assess their grading","MSA applies only if the grades are converted into continuous data"],answer:2,hint:"The book says that even a judgement call has some form of set-up.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 174: 'Even when you are making a judgment call… **You become the instrument** and the result of a decision is recorded'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"So the officers' grading is a measurement system that MSA (attribute MSA) can evaluate."}],final:"Judgement call → person is the instrument → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book says the item measured can be a document or a service scenario.","Incorrect — the judgement itself is already a measurement process; no tool is required.","","Incorrect — attribute measurement systems are assessed with attribute MSA."],bookRef:"Book pp. 173–174"},{id:"sv-2-1274",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"Torque readings taken in a workshop vary noticeably between **cold early mornings and warm afternoons**, although the same parts, wrench, method and person are used. Which element of the measurement system is the MOST likely source?",options:["Environment","Equipment","Procedure","Reference"],answer:0,hint:"Which element of the book's MSA model changed between the readings?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 173: the **environment** is 'the surroundings where the measures are performed'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Only the temperature of the surroundings changed, so the environment is the source."}],final:"Surroundings changed = environment → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the same wrench is used throughout; the changing factor is the surroundings.","Incorrect — the method is unchanged; the procedure is the documented way of testing.","Incorrect — the reference is the calibration standard, which is not mentioned."],bookRef:"Book p. 173"},{id:"sv-2-1275",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"Two team members time the **same recorded calls** with a stopwatch app and get different handling times, because each starts and stops at a different point. The Green Belt writes one operational definition of the start and stop points and trains both. Was this an appropriate action?",options:["Yes, because a clear operational definition and training reduce differences between appraisers","Yes, because this corrects the bias of the stopwatch app","No, because differences between appraisers can only be fixed by buying a new timer","No, because reproducibility errors cannot be reduced"],answer:0,hint:"Poor operational definitions are a classic cause of poor measures.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 177: poor measures result from **poor or non-existent operational definitions**; p. 183: reproducibility, unlike stability, can be eliminated."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A shared definition plus training removes the appraiser-to-appraiser difference."}],final:"Operational definition + training fixes appraiser differences → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the app is not the problem; the error comes from inconsistent start and stop points.","Incorrect — appraiser differences come from the method, so a new timer would not fix them.","Incorrect — the book says reproducibility, unlike stability, can be eliminated."],bookRef:"Book pp. 177, 180, 183"},{id:"sv-2-1276",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"A new optical inspection camera has arrived and will replace the old one for a capability study. What should the Green Belt do FIRST?",options:["Run the capability study straight away","Recalculate the control limits for the process","Ask the customer to widen the specification","Qualify the new camera with an MSA"],answer:3,hint:"One of the book's MSA uses is about new inspection equipment.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 176: MSA provides a way to **qualify new inspection equipment** and to set criteria to accept new measurement systems."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Only after the camera is shown adequate can the capability data be trusted."}],final:"New equipment → qualify with MSA first → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — capability on an unqualified gauge may reflect measurement error rather than the process.","Incorrect — control limits describe the process; the new equipment has not yet been proven.","Incorrect — specifications are the customer's requirement and are unrelated to qualifying a gauge.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 176"},{id:"sv-2-1277",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A gauge with **1 mm** resolution measures parts whose true spread is about **0.2 mm**. Every repeated reading of the same part is identical, so the Green Belt concludes the gauge has **perfect repeatability**. Was this an appropriate conclusion?",options:["Yes, because zero variation between repeated readings shows perfect repeatability","Yes, because identical readings show the gauge has no bias","No, because repeatability can only be judged when several appraisers are used","No, because identical readings are a flag that the resolution may be too coarse"],answer:3,hint:"The book treats readings that are always exactly the same as a warning.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 179: 'If measurements are always exactly the same this is a **flag**' — the gauge may lack proper resolution."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A 1 mm step cannot show 0.2 mm variation, so the 'perfect' repeatability is an illusion."}],final:"Identical readings = resolution flag → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a coarse scale hides variation; zero spread here is not real repeatability.","Incorrect — identical readings say nothing about offset from the true value (bias).","Incorrect — repeatability is studied within one appraiser; several appraisers are needed for reproducibility.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 179",trap:"Zero variation in repeat readings is suspicious, not reassuring."},{id:"sv-2-1278",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A supplier's on-time-delivery figures for the same shipments **do not match** the team's own figures. What should the Green Belt do FIRST?",options:["Run an MSA on both measurement systems before analysing the gap","Use the team's own figures and discard the supplier's","Average the two sets of figures and continue the analysis","Start a root-cause analysis of late deliveries"],answer:0,hint:"The book says customer or supplier data that disagree with yours usually point to one cause.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 177: when data from a customer or supplier do not match yours, 'it is likely a problem with one of the **measurement systems**'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"So validate both measurement systems first."}],final:"Mismatch → check the measurement systems first → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — assuming one side is right ignores that either system may be at fault.","Incorrect — averaging two untrusted measures does not make them trustworthy.","Incorrect — root-cause work on unverified data may chase measurement error."],bookRef:"Book p. 177"},{id:"sv-2-1279",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A call-handling system records **wrap-up time**. A tally of 200 consecutive calls shows:

\`\`\`
Tally for Discrete Variables: Wrap-up time (min)

Wrap-up  Count  Percent
   0       38    19.00
   1      124    62.00
   2       38    19.00
   N=     200
\`\`\`

Wrap-up times are known to vary continuously between about 20 and 100 seconds. What does this output MOST likely indicate?`,options:["The system has a large bias of one minute","The system's resolution is too coarse for the real variation","The process is very stable with almost no variation","The agents have poor reproducibility between them"],answer:1,hint:"Count how many distinct values a continuous measure is producing.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Continuous times spread over 80 s are being recorded in whole minutes, giving only **three distinct values**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"This 'chunky' data shows inadequate resolution; it understates the true variation."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Bias, stability and reproducibility cannot be read from this tally."}],final:"Only three values = poor resolution → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — an offset would shift all values; rounding into three bins is a resolution problem.","","Incorrect — the true times vary widely; the output only looks tight because of rounding.","Incorrect — nothing compares agents; the issue is the recording scale."],bookRef:"Book pp. 174, 179"},{id:"sv-2-1280",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Two inspectors measured the same master part before and after a training course:

\`\`\`
                  Inspector   Mean    StDev
Before training   Ali         5.12    0.030
                  Bea         5.31    0.030
After training    Ali         5.13    0.030
                  Bea         5.14    0.030
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["Training improved repeatability but did not change the difference between inspectors","Training improved both repeatability and the difference between inspectors","Training reduced the difference between inspectors but did not change repeatability","Training made no difference to the measurements"],answer:2,hint:"Within-inspector StDev = repeatability; gap between inspector means = reproducibility.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 176: MSA can **evaluate inspector training effectiveness**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The inspectors' means converged (5.12 vs 5.31 → 5.13 vs 5.14) while each StDev stayed 0.030: reproducibility improved, repeatability unchanged."}],final:"Gap between inspectors closed; spread unchanged → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — each inspector's StDev stayed at 0.030, so repeatability did not change.","Incorrect — each StDev is unchanged at 0.030, so only the gap between inspectors improved.","","Incorrect — the gap between the inspectors' means fell from 0.19 to 0.01."],bookRef:"Book pp. 176, 179–180"},{id:"sv-2-1281",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Two systems measure the same **10.00 s** reference event 30 times each:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: Reading (s)

System       N    Mean   StDev
Manual      30   10.42   0.610
Automated   30   10.00   0.040
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["The manual system is accurate but not precise; the automated system is precise but not accurate","Both systems are accurate, but only the automated system is precise","Both systems are precise, but only the automated system is accurate","The automated system is accurate and precise; the manual system is neither"],answer:3,hint:"Mean versus reference = accuracy; StDev = precision.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 181: if the measurements form a distribution, **accuracy is its mean** and **precision is its variance**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Automated: mean 10.00 (on target), StDev 0.040 (tight). Manual: mean 10.42 (off target), StDev 0.610 (wide)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"This fits the book's remark that computer measurements tend to be best (p. 174)."}],final:"Automated both; manual neither → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the manual mean is 0.42 s from the reference, so it is not accurate, and the automated mean is on target.","Incorrect — the manual mean of 10.42 s is off the reference, so it is not accurate.","Incorrect — the manual StDev of 0.610 s is about fifteen times the automated one, so it is not precise.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 174, 181–182"},{id:"sv-2-1282",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Two devices measure the **same 15 parts** (true part spread about 0.5 mm):

\`\`\`
Paired comparison: Device A − Device B (mm)

N    Mean difference   StDev of difference   P-Value
15        0.003              0.410            0.978
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is BEST supported?`,options:["No offset, but wide part-by-part disagreement, so study precision","The two devices agree, so either can be used for the baseline","Device A has a significant bias compared with Device B","The parts vary too much for the devices to be compared"],answer:0,hint:"Agreement on average is accuracy; agreement part by part needs precision.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 176: MSA is used to **compare measurements between two (or more) measurement devices**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Mean difference 0.003 (P = 0.978) → no systematic offset. StDev of differences 0.410 → individual readings disagree, so repeatability of one or both devices must be studied."}],final:"No offset, poor part-by-part agreement → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the mean difference is near zero, but a 0.410 StDev of differences is large against a 0.5 mm part spread.","Incorrect — P = 0.978 gives no evidence of an offset between the devices.","Incorrect — every part is measured by both devices, so part-to-part variation cancels in the differences."],bookRef:"Book pp. 176, 179–182",trap:"Agreeing on average is not the same as agreeing on each part."},{id:"sv-2-1283",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Green Belt audits four candidate measures for her baseline:

\`\`\`
Measure   Available   Covers all shifts   Helps isolate   Logged with
          weekly?     and agents?         the problem?    context?
M1        Yes         Yes                 Yes             Yes
M2        Yes         No (day shift only) Yes             Yes
M3        No          Yes                 Yes             Yes
M4        Yes         Yes                 No              Yes
\`\`\`

Which measure is NOT **representative**?`,options:["M1","M2","M3","M4"],answer:1,hint:"Representative = detects variation across shifts and people.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 177: a **representative** measure can detect variation across shifts and people."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"M2 covers only the day shift, so it is the measure that is not representative."}],final:"Day shift only = not representative → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — M1 meets all four qualities of an appropriate measure.","","Incorrect — M3 is not sufficient (not available regularly), but it does cover all shifts.","Incorrect — M4 is not relevant (does not help isolate the problem), but it covers all shifts."],bookRef:"Book p. 177"},{id:"sv-2-1284",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A gauge was evaluated before and after repair, using the same master part:

\`\`\`
            Mean    StDev   Bias    P-Value (Bias = 0)
Before      8.031   0.060   0.031   0.008
After       8.030   0.012   0.030   0.000
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["The repair removed the bias but did not improve precision","The repair improved precision but did not remove the bias","The repair improved both precision and accuracy","The repair made no difference to the gauge"],answer:1,hint:"StDev shows precision; bias and its p-value show accuracy.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 176: MSA is used to **evaluate a gauge before and after repair**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"StDev dropped (more precise) but bias stayed at about 0.03 and is significant, so the gauge still needs calibration."}],final:"Spread down, bias unchanged → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the bias is unchanged at about 0.03 and still significant (p = 0.000).","","Incorrect — the bias is still present, so accuracy has not improved.","Incorrect — the StDev fell from 0.060 to 0.012, a clear change in spread."],bookRef:"Book pp. 176, 181–182"},{id:"sv-2-1285",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book, measurement is itself a process, usually involving some set-up, an instrument, a way of recording the value and often several people. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Recall how the book describes the act of measuring.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 174: 'Measurement is a process within itself' — set-up, an instrument, a way of recording and possibly multiple people."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"That is why every step can add error and needs MSA."}],final:"Measurement is a process → answer **True**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the book states that measurement is a process within itself with tasks in sequence."],bookRef:"Book p. 174"},{id:"sv-2-1286",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:3,stem:`A Green Belt is setting up a new metric. Put these activities in the order the book recommends:

- **P** Validate the measurement system with an MSA
- **Q** Answer the question "What do I need to know?"
- **R** Collect and analyse the data
- **S** Choose the metric and the data-collection method`,options:["S → Q → P → R","Q → P → S → R","Q → S → P → R","S → P → Q → R"],answer:2,hint:"Start from the information need; check the gauge before trusting the data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 174: 'What do I need to know?' must be answered **before** considering measurements, metrics or data-collection systems."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Then choose the metric, validate it with MSA, and only then collect and analyse."}],final:"Need → metric → MSA → data → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book says 'What do I need to know?' must be answered before choosing metrics.","Incorrect — the measurement system cannot be validated before the metric is chosen.","","Incorrect — the information need comes first, and MSA follows the choice of metric."],bookRef:"Book pp. 174, 178"},{id:"sv-2-1287",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`A Green Belt uses MSA to improve a measurement system. Put her steps in order:

- **P** Identify the biggest contributor of measurement error
- **Q** Partition the measurement variation into its sources
- **R** Repeat the MSA to confirm the improvement
- **S** Implement changes to the measurement system`,options:["P → Q → S → R","Q → S → P → R","Q → P → R → S","Q → P → S → R"],answer:3,hint:"Partition, prioritise, act, confirm.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 174: MSA partitions variation within the measurement system so an **action plan** can reduce the biggest contributors."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Partition → find the biggest source → change the system → re-run the MSA to confirm."}],final:"Partition → prioritise → act → confirm → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the biggest contributor cannot be found before the variation is partitioned.","Incorrect — changes should target the biggest contributor, so it must be identified first.","Incorrect — the confirming MSA can only follow the changes.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 174"},{id:"sv-2-1288",phase:2,topic:"2.3.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put these Measure- and Analyze-phase activities in the correct order:

- **P** Assess process capability against the specification
- **Q** Test hypotheses about the suspected X's
- **R** Validate the measurement system
- **S** Collect baseline data and check stability`,options:["R → S → P → Q","S → R → P → Q","R → P → S → Q","R → S → Q → P"],answer:0,hint:"Trust the gauge, then the baseline, then capability, then root causes.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 178: MSA comes first — before hypothesis testing, modelling and DOE ('MSA is a Show Stopper')."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Then baseline and stability, capability, and finally Analyze hypothesis tests."}],final:"MSA → stability → capability → hypothesis tests → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — baseline data collected before MSA may be dominated by measurement error.","Incorrect — capability of an unverified-stability baseline is meaningless; stability is checked first.","Incorrect — capability is part of the Measure baseline and comes before Analyze hypothesis tests."],bookRef:"Book pp. 178, 211–217"},{id:"sv-2-1289",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"On a gauge linearity plot, the fitted line of bias against reference value **slopes upward**. What does this mean?",options:["The bias is the same at every reference value","The bias increases as the reference value increases","The bias increases as time goes by","The spread of the readings increases with the reference value"],answer:1,hint:"Read the axes: y = bias, x = reference value.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 183: the linearity plot shows **bias (y)** against **reference value (x)**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"An upward slope means bias grows as the measured value gets larger — a linearity problem."}],final:"Upward slope = bias grows with size → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the same bias everywhere gives a flat line (slope zero).","","Incorrect — the x-axis is the reference value, not time; change over time is stability.","Incorrect — the plot shows bias (location), not the spread of readings."],bookRef:"Book p. 183"},{id:"sv-2-1290",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which question does a **linearity** study answer, according to the book?",options:["Does my gage give the same reading next month as it does today?","Do different appraisers get the same average on the same part?","Does my gage have the same accuracy for all sizes of objects being measured?","Does one appraiser get the same reading when re-measuring a part?"],answer:2,hint:"Linearity is about the measuring range, not time or people.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:`Book p. 183: linearity 'answers the question **"Does my gage have the same accuracy for all sizes of objects being measured?"**'.`},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Time = stability; appraisers = reproducibility; one appraiser re-measuring = repeatability."}],final:"Same accuracy for all sizes = linearity → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a change in accuracy over time is a stability question.","Incorrect — differences between appraisers' averages are reproducibility.","","Incorrect — re-measurement by one appraiser is repeatability."],bookRef:"Book p. 183"},{id:"sv-2-1291",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What does a gauge **stability** study look for?",options:["Changes in bias across the measuring range","Differences between appraisers' averages","The smallest change the gauge can display","Changes in accuracy (bias) over time"],answer:3,hint:"Stability adds the dimension of time to bias.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 183: 'Stability just looks for **changes in the accuracy or Bias over time**'."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Range = linearity; people = reproducibility; scale step = resolution."}],final:"Stability = bias over time → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — change in bias across the range is linearity.","Incorrect — differences between appraisers are reproducibility.","Incorrect — the smallest displayable change is resolution.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 183"},{id:"sv-2-1292",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which type of bias can be corrected with a **single offset adjustment** across the whole range?",options:["Constant bias","Variable bias","Drift","Reproducibility"],answer:0,hint:"One correction only works if the error is the same everywhere.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 182: constant bias is 'off by a constant value' — the same at every reading."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A single re-zero or offset therefore corrects it; variable bias and drift need more than one fixed adjustment."}],final:"Same offset everywhere = constant bias → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — variable bias changes with the setting level, so one offset cannot correct every level.","Incorrect — drift is bias changing over time; one adjustment today will not hold.","Incorrect — reproducibility is appraiser-to-appraiser variation, not a gauge offset."],bookRef:"Book pp. 174, 182"},{id:"sv-2-1293",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"A gauge stability study measures **one master part** at regular intervals. Which accuracy problem can this study NOT reveal?",options:["A gradual drift in bias over the weeks","A change in bias across the measuring range","A sudden shift in bias after the gauge is moved","A bias that was present from the first day"],answer:1,hint:"One master part means one point in the measuring range.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 183: stability = bias over time; linearity = difference in bias **throughout the range**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"With only one master value, the study cannot see how bias differs at other sizes — that needs a linearity study."}],final:"One master cannot show linearity → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — gradual drift over time is exactly what a stability study is designed to show.","","Incorrect — a sudden shift in the master readings appears on the stability chart.","Incorrect — comparing the master readings with its reference value shows a bias present from day one."],bookRef:"Book p. 183",trap:"A stability study and a linearity study answer different questions."},{id:"sv-2-1294",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"How does an uncorrected **bias** affect baseline data collected with the gauge?",options:["It widens the observed spread of the process data","It turns a normal distribution into a skewed one","It shifts every reading, misplacing the process average","It hides special causes on the control chart"],answer:2,hint:"Bias is a location error.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 173: bias, linearity and stability affect **location (the average)**, not spread."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A biased gauge moves all readings, so the process may look off-centre when it is not — or centred when it is not."}],final:"Bias moves the average → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — extra spread comes from poor precision (repeatability and reproducibility), not bias.","Incorrect — a fixed offset moves the whole distribution; it does not change its shape.","","Incorrect — a constant offset moves the centre line too; it does not mask signals by itself."],bookRef:"Book pp. 173, 176"},{id:"sv-2-1295",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why does the book say bias can **never be fully eliminated**?",options:["Because appraisers use different techniques","Because specifications change over time","Because reference values are never known","Because of material wear and tear"],answer:3,hint:"Think about what physically happens to a gauge with use.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 182: calibration can minimise and control bias, but 'Bias can never be eliminated due to **material wear and tear**'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"That is why calibration is periodic rather than one-off."}],final:"Wear and tear keeps bias coming back → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — differences in appraiser technique cause reproducibility error, not gauge bias.","Incorrect — specifications are the customer's requirement and do not create gauge bias.","Incorrect — bias is measured against a known reference value; without one it could not be estimated.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 182"},{id:"sv-2-1296",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A gauge has a **stability** problem. On a chart of daily readings of the same master part, which feature changes over time?",options:["The average level of the readings (the bias)","The reference value of the master part","The specification limits of the product","The number of distinct categories of the gauge"],answer:0,hint:"Stability is bias characterised as a function of time.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 183: stability 'looks for changes in the accuracy or **Bias** over time'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"So the level of the master readings moves away from the reference value as time passes."}],final:"Unstable gauge → master readings' level moves → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the master's reference value is fixed; the gauge's readings of it are what move.","Incorrect — specification limits are the customer's requirement and do not change with the gauge.","Incorrect — distinct categories come from a Gage R&R study, not from a stability chart."],bookRef:"Book p. 183"},{id:"sv-2-1297",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In a **Type 1 gage study**, how is the bias of the gauge assessed?",options:["Ten parts are measured twice each by three operators","One reference part is measured many times and the average is compared with its reference value","Several reference parts across the range are measured and bias is plotted against size","Production parts are measured once each and compared with the specification"],answer:1,hint:"A Type 1 study is the simplest bias check.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A Type 1 gage study uses **one reference part** measured repeatedly; bias = average reading − reference value (book p. 182)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Gage R&R = precision; linearity study = bias across the range; capability = process."}],final:"Type 1 = one reference part, many readings → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — several operators measuring several parts is a crossed Gage R&R (precision).","","Incorrect — references across the range with a bias plot is a linearity study.","Incorrect — comparing production parts with specifications is capability, with no reference value."],bookRef:"Book pp. 173, 182"},{id:"sv-2-1298",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which Minitab study estimates **both linearity and bias** in one analysis?",options:["Gage R&R Study (Crossed)","Attribute Agreement Analysis","Gage Linearity and Bias Study","Capability Analysis (Normal)"],answer:2,hint:"Match the study to the error it estimates.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **Gage Linearity and Bias Study** regresses bias on reference value (linearity) and tests the bias at each reference (bias)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Gage R&R = precision; attribute agreement = attribute MSA; capability = process."}],final:"Linearity + bias = Gage Linearity and Bias Study → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a crossed Gage R&R partitions repeatability and reproducibility (precision).","Incorrect — attribute agreement analysis assesses pass/fail or category judgements.","","Incorrect — capability analysis compares the process with its specifications."],bookRef:"Book pp. 173–174, 181–183"},{id:"sv-2-1299",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"According to the book, why do bias, linearity or stability problems matter to the **customer**?",options:["They make the customer's specification limits narrower","They increase the customer's own repeatability error","They affect the customer only when attribute data are used","They may lead the customer to misjudge the product"],answer:3,hint:"Who reads and acts on our measurements?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 176: a measurement system with bias, linearity or stability problems may mean 'your customer may be making the **wrong interpretations** about your product or service'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Accuracy errors mislead everyone who relies on the data."}],final:"Accuracy errors mislead the customer → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — specifications are set by the customer and are not changed by our gauge.","Incorrect — our gauge's offset does not change the spread of the customer's own measurements.","Incorrect — accuracy problems distort continuous data as well as attribute data.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 176"},{id:"sv-2-1300",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book, when can a measurement system be described as **accurate**?",options:["When it does not have a bias, linearity or stability problem","When its repeatability and reproducibility are both small","When it has a valid calibration certificate","When it shows five or more distinct categories"],answer:0,hint:"Accuracy has three components in the book.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 176: a measurement system is accurate, 'meaning it does not have a **bias, linearity or stability** problem'."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Repeatability, reproducibility and ndc are about precision."}],final:"Accurate = no bias, linearity or stability problem → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — small repeatability and reproducibility make a system precise, not accurate.","Incorrect — a certificate records a past calibration; bias, linearity and stability can still be present.","Incorrect — distinct categories describe how well the system separates parts (precision), not accuracy."],bookRef:"Book p. 176"},{id:"sv-2-1301",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Bias is a component of which property of a measurement system?",options:["Accuracy","Precision","Repeatability","Reproducibility"],answer:0,hint:"Location errors versus spread errors.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 182: 'Bias is a component of **Accuracy**'."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Repeatability and reproducibility are the two parts of precision."}],final:"Bias → accuracy → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — precision is spread, split into repeatability and reproducibility.","Incorrect — repeatability is a component of precision (within one appraiser).","Incorrect — reproducibility is a component of precision (between appraisers)."],bookRef:"Book p. 182"},{id:"sv-2-1302",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"In a Minitab Gage Linearity output, what does the **Constant** row of the fitted line represent?",options:["The average bias over all reference values","The bias predicted at a reference value of zero","The amount the bias changes per unit of reference value","The repeatability of the gauge at each reference value"],answer:1,hint:"The fitted line is bias = constant + slope × reference.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 183: the linearity line is fitted with bias (y) against reference value (x)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The Constant is the intercept — the bias the line predicts where the reference value is zero; the Slope tells whether bias changes across the range."}],final:"Constant = intercept (bias at reference 0) → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the average bias is shown in the 'Average' row of the Gage Bias table.","","Incorrect — the change in bias per unit of reference value is the Slope.","Incorrect — a linearity study does not report repeatability; that comes from Gage R&R."],bookRef:"Book p. 183"},{id:"sv-2-1303",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In a Minitab Gage Bias table, what does the **Average** row show?",options:["The bias at the middle reference value only","The average reading of all appraisers in a Gage R&R","The bias averaged over all the reference values studied","The average slope of the linearity line"],answer:2,hint:"The Gage Bias table has one row per reference plus a summary row.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Gage Bias table lists the bias at each reference value, plus an **Average** row for the overall bias."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"An average near zero can still hide opposite biases at the ends, so read the individual rows and the slope too."}],final:"Average row = overall bias → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — each reference value has its own row; the Average row combines them.","Incorrect — a Gage Bias table compares readings with references, not appraisers.","","Incorrect — the slope is a single value in the Gage Linearity section."],bookRef:"Book pp. 182–183"},{id:"sv-2-1304",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"A gauge's bias **increases with the size** of the part measured. How does this affect a capability study using the gauge?",options:["It shifts every reading by the same amount but leaves the spread unchanged","It has no effect, because capability uses only the process standard deviation","It affects only attribute capability, not continuous capability","It distorts the measured spread, so the capability indices misrepresent the process"],answer:3,hint:"Large parts read even larger and small parts relatively smaller.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 183: linearity = difference in bias throughout the range."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"If bias grows with size, the readings are stretched (or squeezed), so the observed spread — and Cp, Cpk — no longer reflect the real process."}],final:"Linearity error distorts spread and capability → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — that describes a constant bias; here the offset differs with size.","Incorrect — any change in apparent spread or location feeds straight into Cp and Cpk.","Incorrect — measurement errors affect continuous capability directly.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 176, 183"},{id:"sv-2-1305",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"In a Minitab **Gage Bias** table, which column shows whether the bias at a reference value is statistically significant?",options:["P","%Bias","Bias","Reference"],answer:0,hint:"Significance is always read from one familiar statistic.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each row of the Gage Bias table tests H₀: bias = 0; the **P** column gives the p-value."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"P < 0.05 → reject H₀ → the bias at that reference is significant."}],final:"Significance → P column → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — %Bias expresses the size of the bias relative to process variation, not its significance.","Incorrect — the Bias column gives the size of the offset, not whether it is significant.","Incorrect — the Reference column lists the master values that were measured."],bookRef:"Book pp. 173–174, 181–183"},{id:"sv-2-1306",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about **averaging** several readings of the same part is CORRECT?",options:["It removes bias but does not reduce random spread","It reduces random spread but does not remove bias","It removes both bias and random spread","It corrects linearity across the range"],answer:1,hint:"Which error is shared by every reading?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Bias is an offset of the average from the true value (book p. 182); random spread is precision."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Averaging smooths the random part only; the offset is in every reading, so it stays. Only calibration controls bias."}],final:"Averaging cuts spread, not bias → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — an offset is present in every reading, so it is still there in the average.","","Incorrect — averaging cannot remove an offset that every reading shares.","Incorrect — bias that changes with size stays in every average at that size."],bookRef:"Book pp. 173, 182"},{id:"sv-2-1307",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about **variable bias** is CORRECT?",options:["It is the kind of bias a single re-zero will remove","It is the kind of bias caused by differences between appraisers","It is the kind of bias a linearity study is designed to detect","It is the kind of bias that appears only over time"],answer:2,hint:"Variable bias changes with the setting level.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 174: variable bias — 'amount of Bias changes with setting levels'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Bias that differs across levels is exactly what linearity measures (p. 183)."}],final:"Variable bias ↔ linearity → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a single offset only removes constant bias.","Incorrect — differences between appraisers are reproducibility, not bias in the gauge.","","Incorrect — bias changing over time is stability (drift)."],bookRef:"Book pp. 174, 183"},{id:"sv-2-1308",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which statement about **%Bias** in a Minitab Gage Bias table is CORRECT?",options:["It is the p-value of the test that the bias is zero","It is the percentage of readings above the reference value","It expresses the slope of the linearity line as a percentage","It gives the bias as a percentage of process variation"],answer:3,hint:"Size and significance are shown in different columns.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Minitab reports %Bias = |bias| relative to the process variation × 100, beside the P column that tests significance."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Slope × 100 is %Linearity; P gives significance."}],final:"%Bias = bias as % of process variation → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the p-value is in the P column; %Bias describes size, not significance.","Incorrect — %Bias is about the size of the offset, not a count of readings.","Incorrect — the slope as a percentage is %Linearity.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 182–183"},{id:"sv-2-1309",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about the effect of a **constant bias** on a process capability study is CORRECT?",options:["It shifts the measured mean, so Cpk is distorted while Cp is unaffected","It widens the measured spread, so Cp is distorted while Cpk is unaffected","It distorts Cp and Cpk equally because both use the mean","It has no effect because capability uses only within-subgroup variation"],answer:0,hint:"Bias is a location error; recall which index uses the mean.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 173: bias affects **location (the average)**, not spread."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Cp depends on spread only, so it is unchanged; Cpk depends on where the mean sits, so the offset distorts it."}],final:"Constant bias moves the mean → Cpk wrong, Cp unchanged → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a constant offset moves the location; it does not widen the spread.","Incorrect — Cp uses only the spread and the specification width, not the mean.","Incorrect — Cpk uses the distance from the mean to the nearest limit, which an offset changes."],bookRef:"Book pp. 173, 216",trap:"Bias never shows up in Cp."},{id:"sv-2-1310",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about **%Linearity** is CORRECT?",options:["%Linearity depends only on the average bias, not on the slope","A slope of bias near zero gives a %Linearity near zero","A large average bias always gives a large %Linearity","%Linearity measures how much repeated readings vary"],answer:1,hint:"Recall which quantity the book multiplies by 100.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 183: **%Linearity = |Slope| × 100**, where the slope is from bias regressed on reference value."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A slope near zero means bias barely changes across the range, so %Linearity is near zero."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Average bias drives %Bias, not %Linearity."}],final:"%Linearity follows |slope| → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book defines %Linearity from the absolute slope, not from the average bias.","","Incorrect — a large but constant bias gives a flat line, so %Linearity stays near zero.","Incorrect — variation of repeated readings is precision (repeatability)."],bookRef:"Book p. 183"},{id:"sv-2-1311",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Consider these statements about bias.

1. Calibration eliminates bias permanently.
2. Bias is a component of precision.
3. Bias is the deviation of the measured value from the actual value.
4. A scale that reads 3 lb with nothing on it shows constant bias.

Which statements are CORRECT?`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:2,hint:"Check each statement against the book's bias page.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 182: bias = deviation of the measured value from the actual value; constant bias example = 3 lb scale; calibration minimises but never eliminates bias; bias is a component of accuracy."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"So 3 and 4 are true; 1 and 2 are false."}],final:"Statements 3 and 4 are true → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 1 is false (wear and tear means bias can never be eliminated) and 2 is false (bias is accuracy).","Incorrect — includes 2; bias is a component of accuracy, not precision.","","Incorrect — includes 1; calibration minimises and controls bias but cannot eliminate it."],bookRef:"Book p. 182"},{id:"sv-2-1312",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"statements",difficulty:3,stem:`Consider these statements about a Minitab Gage Linearity and Bias output.

1. A significant Slope indicates a linearity problem.
2. A high R-Sq proves that the average bias is significant.
3. The P-value of the Constant row tests linearity.
4. A bias row with P below 0.05 shows significant bias at that reference value.

Which statements are CORRECT?`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:3,hint:"Slope → linearity; bias rows → bias; R-Sq is not a p-value.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 183: linearity = bias changing across the range, so the **Slope** is the linearity test; each Gage Bias row tests bias = 0."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"So 1 and 4 are true; 2 confuses R-Sq with significance and 3 confuses the Constant with the Slope."}],final:"Statements 1 and 4 are true → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — includes 2; R-Sq describes how well the line fits, not whether the bias is significant.","Incorrect — 2 and 3 are both false: R-Sq is not a significance test and the Slope, not the Constant, tests linearity.","Incorrect — includes 3; linearity is judged from the Slope row.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 183"},{id:"sv-2-1313",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Consider these statements about the consequences of accuracy errors.

1. A biased gauge can make a centred process look off-centre.
2. A gauge with poor linearity can be right at one size and wrong at another.
3. A constant bias widens the observed spread of the data.
4. Calibration improves the repeatability of a gauge.

Which statements are CORRECT?`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:0,hint:"Accuracy errors move the location; precision errors change the spread.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 173: bias, linearity and stability affect **location**; calibration minimises bias (p. 182)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"So 1 (bias misplaces the mean) and 2 (linearity varies with size) are true; 3 and 4 treat accuracy as spread."}],final:"Statements 1 and 2 are true → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — includes 3; a constant bias moves the location, not the spread.","Incorrect — 3 and 4 are both false: bias does not widen spread and calibration does not improve repeatability.","Incorrect — includes 4; calibration controls bias, a location error, not repeatability."],bookRef:"Book pp. 173, 182–183"},{id:"sv-2-1314",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

Bias is the deviation of the measured value from the [?] value.`,options:["average","nominal","specified","true"],answer:3,hint:"Bias compares the reading with reality.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 182: 'Bias is defined as the deviation of the measured value from the **actual** value' — the true (reference) value."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Average, nominal and specified values are not the true value of the item."}],final:"Deviation from the true value → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the average of the readings is what is compared; it is not the value bias is measured from.","Incorrect — the nominal is the design target, not the actual value of what is measured.","Incorrect — a specified value is a requirement; bias is judged against the actual value.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 182"},{id:"sv-2-1315",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

Linearity tells you how [?] your measurements are through the expected range of the measurements.`,options:["accurate","precise","repeatable","reproducible"],answer:0,hint:"Linearity is one of the components of a location property.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 183: linearity 'tells you how **accurate** your measurements are through the expected range'."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Precise, repeatable and reproducible describe spread, not location."}],final:"Linearity = accuracy across the range → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — precision is the spread of repeated readings; linearity concerns bias (accuracy).","Incorrect — repeatability is spread within one appraiser, a precision component.","Incorrect — reproducibility is the difference between appraisers, a precision component."],bookRef:"Book p. 183"},{id:"sv-2-1316",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing words in the following sentence.

In the book's formula, Linearity = |Slope| × [?].`,options:["number of trials","process variation","reference value","tolerance width"],answer:1,hint:"The book gives two formulas: one multiplies by 100, the other by a measure of spread.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 183: **Linearity = |Slope| × Process Variation** and %Linearity = |Slope| × 100."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Trials, reference value and tolerance are not in the formula."}],final:"Linearity = |slope| × process variation → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the number of trials affects how well bias is estimated, not the linearity formula.","","Incorrect — the reference value is the x-axis of the plot, not the multiplier.","Incorrect — the book's formula uses process variation, not the tolerance width."],bookRef:"Book p. 183"},{id:"sv-2-1317",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:2,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

[?] is the difference in bias values throughout the measurement range in which the gauge is intended to be used.`,options:["Drift","Linearity","Reproducibility","Stability"],answer:1,hint:"The keyword is 'throughout the measurement range'.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 183: '**Linearity** is defined as the difference in Bias values throughout the measurement range in which the gauge is intended to be used'."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Drift and stability are about time; reproducibility is about appraisers."}],final:"Across the range = linearity → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — drift is the book's picture of stability: bias changing over time.","","Incorrect — reproducibility is the variation between appraisers' averages.","Incorrect — stability is the change in bias over time."],bookRef:"Book p. 183"},{id:"sv-2-1318",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which of these studies does NOT need a **reference value** (master) for the parts measured?",options:["Bias study","Linearity study","Reproducibility study","Stability study"],answer:2,hint:"Which error is defined by comparing appraisers with each other rather than with the truth?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 173: accuracy errors (bias, linearity, stability) all compare readings with a **master reference value**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Reproducibility compares appraisers' averages with each other, so no true value is needed."}],final:"Reproducibility compares appraisers, not truth → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — bias is the measured average minus the reference value, so a reference is essential.","Incorrect — linearity is bias across the range, so references at several sizes are needed.","","Incorrect — stability tracks the bias of a master part over time."],bookRef:"Book pp. 173, 180–183"},{id:"sv-2-1319",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which result is NOT reported in a Minitab **Gage Linearity and Bias** study?",options:["The slope and its p-value","The bias at each reference value","%Linearity","Repeatability %Study Var"],answer:3,hint:"A linearity and bias study is about accuracy; one option is a precision measure.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The study reports the fitted line (Constant, Slope, P), Linearity, %Linearity and a Gage Bias table."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Repeatability %Study Var is a precision result from a Gage R&R, so it is not in this output."}],final:"%Study Var belongs to Gage R&R → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the slope and its p-value form the Gage Linearity section.","Incorrect — the bias at each reference value forms the Gage Bias table.","Incorrect — %Linearity is printed under the fitted line.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 183, 185"},{id:"sv-2-1320",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which finding would NOT indicate a **bias** problem?",options:["Repeated readings of a master scatter widely, centred on the reference value","The average of repeated readings of a master is 0.3 above the reference value","A scale reads 2 kg with nothing on it","A bias test on a master part gives P = 0.001"],answer:0,hint:"Bias is about where the readings centre, not how widely they spread.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 182: bias = deviation of the measured value from the actual value — a **location** error."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Wide scatter centred on the reference is a precision problem with no bias."}],final:"Wide but centred scatter = precision, not bias → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — an average 0.3 above the reference is an offset from the true value, which is bias.","Incorrect — reading 2 kg when empty is the book's example of constant bias.","Incorrect — P below 0.05 rejects zero bias, so the bias is significant."],bookRef:"Book pp. 179–182",trap:"Poor precision is not bias."},{id:"sv-2-1321",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"A gauge is checked with reference parts across its range. Which finding would NOT indicate a **linearity** problem?",options:["Negative bias at the low end and positive bias at the high end","The same offset of 0.2 mm at every reference size","A significant slope when bias is regressed on reference value","Bias that grows steadily as the reference value increases"],answer:1,hint:"Linearity is about whether bias changes across the range, not whether it exists.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 183: linearity evaluates 'if any Bias is **consistent** throughout the measurement range'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A constant 0.2 mm offset at every size is constant bias — fix it by calibration; linearity is fine."}],final:"Constant offset = bias, not linearity → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — bias changing sign across the range is a classic linearity problem.","","Incorrect — a significant slope of bias on reference is the statistical sign of a linearity problem.","Incorrect — bias growing with size means it is not consistent across the range."],bookRef:"Book pp. 182–183",trap:"Any offset is not automatically a linearity problem."},{id:"sv-2-1322",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A billing team checks its invoice calculator by working out the **expected amount by hand** for 40 invoices from the tariff rules. The calculator's amount is exactly **OMR 0.50 higher** on every invoice. Which way of assessing accuracy is used, and what does it show?",options:["Measurement of a known standard; it shows poor repeatability","Comparison with another known method; it shows a linearity problem","Prediction of a theoretical value; it shows a constant bias","Comparison between appraisers; it shows poor reproducibility"],answer:2,hint:"The reference values came from the tariff rules, not from a standard or another instrument.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 181: accuracy can be assessed by a known standard, another known method, or **prediction of a theoretical value**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Hand-calculated amounts are theoretical values; the same +0.50 on every invoice is a constant bias."}],final:"Theoretical value; constant bias → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — no physical standard was measured, and an identical offset is not a spread problem.","Incorrect — the expected amounts were calculated from rules, and the offset does not change with invoice size.","","Incorrect — no appraisers are compared; the calculator is compared with calculated values."],bookRef:"Book pp. 181–182"},{id:"sv-2-1323",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A team wants to know whether its torque analyser **still reads a reference joint the same as it did when it was calibrated three months ago**. Which study answers this question?",options:["A linearity study","A crossed Gage R&R study","A capability study","A stability study"],answer:3,hint:"The question is about the same reference over time.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 183: stability looks for changes in accuracy or bias **over time**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Measuring the same reference at intervals since calibration is a stability study."}],final:"Same reference over time = stability study → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a linearity study compares bias across sizes, not across months.","Incorrect — a crossed Gage R&R splits repeatability and reproducibility at one time.","Incorrect — capability compares the process with its specifications.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 183"},{id:"sv-2-1324",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A customer's incoming inspection rejects shafts that the supplier passed. Both measure the **same certified master**: the customer's gauge reads it correctly, while the supplier's gauge reads it **0.10 mm low** every time. What is the MOST likely explanation for the disagreement?",options:["The supplier's gauge has a bias","The customer's gauge has poor repeatability","The supplier's process is unstable","The two companies use different specification limits"],answer:0,hint:"Check each gauge against the certified master.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 182: bias = deviation of the measured value from the actual value; p. 176: accuracy errors lead customers to wrong interpretations."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The supplier's gauge consistently reads the master 0.10 mm low, so it passes shafts that are really too large or too small."}],final:"Supplier gauge reads master low = bias → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the customer's gauge reads the master correctly; no scatter problem is described.","Incorrect — the master is the same object for both, so the process cannot explain the gap.","Incorrect — nothing suggests different specifications; the gauges disagree on the same master."],bookRef:"Book pp. 176, 182"},{id:"sv-2-1325",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:`A pressure gauge is used from **10 to 200 bar**. A technician calibrated it at **100 bar** only and says: *"It's calibrated, so it's accurate across the whole range."* What is the MAIN concern?`,options:["The gauge may not be repeatable when one person re-measures","Bias may differ elsewhere in the range (linearity)","Different technicians may read the dial differently","The gauge's resolution is too coarse at 100 bar"],answer:1,hint:"One calibration point says nothing about bias elsewhere in the range.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 183: linearity asks 'Does my gage have the same accuracy for **all sizes**?'"},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A single mid-range calibration cannot show whether bias changes at 10 or 200 bar — a linearity study is needed."}],final:"Single-point calibration ignores linearity → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — repeatability is a precision issue; the claim is about accuracy across the range.","","Incorrect — differences between technicians are reproducibility, not the range claim.","Incorrect — nothing suggests a resolution problem; the issue is bias at other pressures."],bookRef:"Book p. 183"},{id:"sv-2-1326",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"An automated call-quality scorer is tested on a recording whose **expert reference score is 80**. Scored ten times, it returns **72 every time**. Which error must be corrected?",options:["Bias","Linearity","Repeatability","Stability"],answer:0,hint:"Compare the readings with the reference value, then look at their spread.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 182: bias = deviation of the measured value from the actual value."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The scorer is consistent (no spread) but always 8 points below the reference — a bias that its scoring model must be calibrated to remove."}],final:"Consistent but off the reference = bias → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — only one reference level was tested, so a change in bias across the range cannot be seen.","Incorrect — ten identical scores show no spread on repeats; the problem is the offset from 80.","Incorrect — nothing shows a change over time; the scorer is wrong from the start."],bookRef:"Book pp. 181–182"},{id:"sv-2-1327",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A gauge with a known bias is calibrated. Afterwards, repeated readings of the master are **centred on the reference value but still scatter widely**. What remains to be addressed?",options:["Bias","Linearity","Repeatability","Stability"],answer:2,hint:"Calibration fixes location; what is left when the location is right?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 182: calibration minimises and controls **bias**; precision is the spread of repeated readings (p. 179)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Centred readings mean bias is fixed; wide scatter is a repeatability problem still to be solved."}],final:"Centred but scattered = precision problem → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the readings are now centred on the reference, so the bias has been corrected.","Incorrect — only one master was measured, and the issue is scatter, not bias across the range.","","Incorrect — nothing describes a change over time; the problem is scatter at one moment."],bookRef:"Book pp. 179, 182"},{id:"sv-2-1328",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"A gauge reads **0.3 mm high** at every size. To avoid rejecting good parts, the Green Belt proposes to **widen the specification limits by 0.3 mm**. Was this an appropriate action?",options:["Yes, because the wider limits exactly cancel the bias","Yes, because specification limits can be adjusted to suit the gauge","No, because the bias should be removed by calibrating the gauge","No, because a constant bias can only be removed by replacing the gauge"],answer:2,hint:"Fix the measurement, not the requirement.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 182: calibration procedures **minimise and control bias**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A constant 0.3 mm offset is corrected by calibrating (re-zeroing) the gauge; changing the specification hides the error and misleads the customer."}],final:"Calibrate; never move the specification → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — specifications are the customer's requirement; the gauge must be corrected, not the requirement.","Incorrect — specification limits come from the customer (VOC) and are not set to suit a gauge.","","Incorrect — replacement is for linearity problems; a constant offset is corrected by calibration."],bookRef:"Book p. 182"},{id:"sv-2-1329",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:'After calibrating a gauge, a technician states: *"The bias has now been eliminated permanently, so no further checks are needed."* Was this an appropriate conclusion?',options:["Yes, because calibration sets the bias to exactly zero","Yes, because bias only matters during a Gage R&R study","No, because bias is a precision error that calibration cannot touch","No, because wear and tear means bias must be monitored"],answer:3,hint:"Recall the book's words on what calibration can and cannot do.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 182: calibration can **minimise and control** bias, but bias 'can never be eliminated due to material wear and tear'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Periodic checks — starting with a stability study after calibration (p. 183) — are still required."}],final:"Calibration controls bias, never eliminates it → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — calibration minimises and controls bias within limits; it does not remove it for good.","Incorrect — bias affects every reading the gauge takes, not just a study.","Incorrect — bias is an accuracy (location) error, and calibration is exactly what controls it.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 182–183"},{id:"sv-2-1330",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"An electronic flow meter shows a bias that **changes with the flow setting**: small at low flow, larger at high flow. What is the BEST way to correct it?",options:["Apply one offset correction measured at low flow","Retrain the operators who read the meter","Add more trials per part to the Gage R&R","Calibrate at several points across the operating range"],answer:3,hint:"The book has a name for bias that changes with setting levels.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 174: **variable bias** — typical of electronic equipment — 'amount of Bias changes with setting levels'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Because the bias differs across the range, it must be corrected at several points, not with one offset."}],final:"Variable bias → multi-point calibration → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a single offset suits constant bias; this bias varies with setting.","Incorrect — the error comes from the instrument at different settings, not from people.","Incorrect — more trials sharpen the precision estimate but do not correct bias.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 174, 183"},{id:"sv-2-1331",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"For a linearity study, a Green Belt sets the **reference values** of five parts by measuring them with the **same gauge** that is being studied. Was this an appropriate action?",options:["Yes, because using the same gauge keeps the study consistent","Yes, because any reference value is acceptable if the parts span the range","No, because the reference values must come from a more accurate standard or method","No, because a linearity study needs only one reference part"],answer:2,hint:"Bias = measured average minus a value known to be true.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 173: accuracy compares the observed average with 'the true average based on a **master reference value**'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"References measured with the studied gauge carry its own bias, so the study would show near-zero bias whatever the truth."}],final:"References must come from a better method → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — if the gauge sets its own references, its bias is built into them and becomes invisible.","Incorrect — spanning the range is necessary but the references must also be close to the true values.","","Incorrect — linearity needs several references across the range; one part is a bias or stability study."],bookRef:"Book pp. 173, 181–183"},{id:"sv-2-1332",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A gauge with a linearity problem was serviced. The Gage Linearity and Bias study was repeated:

\`\`\`
                    Slope     P(Slope)   Average bias   P(Average)
Before service    -0.0310     0.000        0.105         0.000
After service     -0.0008     0.790        0.098         0.000
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["Linearity is fixed, but a significant bias remains to be calibrated out","The service fixed both the linearity problem and the bias","The service fixed the bias, but the linearity problem remains","The service made no difference to the gauge"],answer:0,hint:"Read the slope test for linearity and the average-bias test for bias, before and after.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 183: linearity problems often need maintenance; p. 182: calibration controls bias."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"After service the slope is not significant (P = 0.790) → linearity fixed; average bias 0.098 (P = 0.000) → still biased → calibrate."}],final:"Linearity fixed, bias remains → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the average bias after service is 0.098 with P = 0.000, so it is still significant.","Incorrect — the slope P-value is now 0.790, so linearity is fixed; the bias is what remains.","Incorrect — the slope changed from −0.0310 (P = 0.000) to −0.0008 (P = 0.790)."],bookRef:"Book pp. 182–183"},{id:"sv-2-1333",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A depth gauge is checked against five references:

\`\`\`
Gage Bias
Reference    Bias    %Bias      P
Average     0.022     0.4   0.031
2           0.004     0.1   0.710
4          -0.003     0.1   0.802
6           0.006     0.1   0.624
8           0.010     0.2   0.440
10          0.092     1.5   0.000
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is BEST supported?`,options:["The gauge has the same significant bias at every reference value","The gauge is biased only at the top of the range","The gauge has no significant bias anywhere in its range","The gauge is biased at the low end but accurate at the top"],answer:1,hint:"Read the P column row by row.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each Gage Bias row tests bias = 0 at that reference; P < 0.05 → significant."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Only reference 10 (P = 0.000) is significant; bias is not consistent across the range, so linearity at the top end needs attention."}],final:"Significant bias only at the top reference → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — references 2 to 8 have p-values from 0.44 to 0.80, so their bias is not significant.","","Incorrect — the reference 10 row has P = 0.000, so the bias there is significant.","Incorrect — this reverses the output: the only significant bias is at reference 10."],bookRef:"Book p. 183"},{id:"sv-2-1334",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A new rapid moisture meter is compared with the **laboratory oven method** on the same 12 grain samples:

\`\`\`
Paired comparison: Meter − Lab (% moisture)

N    Mean difference   StDev of difference   P-Value
12        -0.80               0.06            0.000
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["The meter agrees with the lab method, so it has no bias","The meter has poor repeatability but no bias","The meter reads significantly low, so it has a bias","The lab method should be corrected to match the meter"],answer:2,hint:"Accuracy can be assessed by comparison with another known method.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 181: accuracy can be assessed by '**comparison with another known measurement method**'."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A consistent −0.80 difference with P = 0.000 means the meter reads low — a bias to correct by calibration."}],final:"Consistent −0.80 vs lab = bias → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the mean difference is −0.80 with P = 0.000, so the two methods do not agree.","Incorrect — the differences are very consistent (StDev 0.06); the problem is the offset.","","Incorrect — the established lab method is the known reference the meter is compared with."],bookRef:"Book pp. 181–182"},{id:"sv-2-1335",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A gauge was checked on the same master part before and after calibration:

\`\`\`
               Mean    StDev   Bias    P-Value (Bias = 0)
Before cal.   12.40    0.050   0.40    0.000
After cal.    12.005   0.051   0.005   0.560
\`\`\`

(Reference value = 12.00)

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["Calibration improved both the bias and the precision","Calibration improved the precision but left the bias unchanged","Calibration made the gauge less precise and still biased","Calibration removed the significant bias but did not change the spread"],answer:3,hint:"Calibration acts on location, not spread.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 182: calibration minimises and controls **bias** (a location error)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Bias fell from 0.40 (p = 0.000) to 0.005 (p = 0.560); StDev unchanged at about 0.05 → spread untouched."}],final:"Bias removed, spread unchanged → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — StDev is 0.050 before and 0.051 after, so precision did not improve.","Incorrect — StDev hardly moved, while bias fell from 0.40 to 0.005.","Incorrect — the after-calibration bias is not significant (p = 0.560).",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 173, 182"},{id:"sv-2-1336",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A gauge's bias on the same master part was checked at the start of each month:

\`\`\`
Month   Mean reading   Reference   Bias    P-Value (Bias = 0)
Jan        20.052        20.000    0.052      0.000
Feb        20.049        20.000    0.049      0.000
Mar        20.051        20.000    0.051      0.000
Apr        20.050        20.000    0.050      0.000
May        20.052        20.000    0.052      0.000
Jun        20.049        20.000    0.049      0.000
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is BEST supported?`,options:["The gauge is stable but has a significant bias to calibrate out","The gauge is drifting upward, so it has a stability problem","The gauge has no bias because the readings are consistent","The gauge has a linearity problem because the bias is not zero"],answer:0,hint:"Separate 'does the bias change over time?' from 'is there a bias?'.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 183: stability = bias over time; p. 182: bias = deviation from the actual value."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The bias hardly changes month to month (stable) but is about 0.05 with P = 0.000 every month (biased) → calibrate."}],final:"Stable but biased → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the bias stays between 0.049 and 0.052 every month, so there is no drift.","Incorrect — consistent readings show stability, not accuracy; every month's bias is significant.","Incorrect — only one master value was used, so linearity cannot be judged."],bookRef:"Book pp. 182–183",trap:"A stable gauge is not necessarily an accurate one."},{id:"sv-2-1337",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:1,stem:`Two scales weigh the same **5.000 kg** reference mass 20 times each:

\`\`\`
Scale   Mean    StDev   Bias    P-Value (Bias = 0)
A       5.031   0.004   0.031   0.000
B       5.001   0.004   0.001   0.278
\`\`\`

Which scale needs calibration?`,options:["Scale A only","Scale B only","Both scales","Neither scale"],answer:0,hint:"Calibration targets significant bias.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"P < 0.05 → reject bias = 0 → the scale is biased and needs calibration."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Scale A: P = 0.000 → biased. Scale B: P = 0.278 → fail to reject. The equal StDevs show both are equally precise."}],final:"Only Scale A has significant bias → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — Scale B's bias p-value is 0.278, so there is no evidence of bias.","Incorrect — only Scale A shows a significant bias.","Incorrect — Scale A's bias p-value is 0.000, so its bias is significant."],bookRef:"Book p. 182"},{id:"sv-2-1338",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"Bias can occur only in manufacturing gauges, not in transactional measurement systems such as database queries. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Think of the book's database-query example.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 181: in transactional processes the measurement system can be a database query, and its accuracy must be checked against known reference points."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A query or calculator can consistently over- or under-state values — that is bias, so the statement is false."}],final:"Transactional systems can be biased too → answer **False**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book shows transactional measurement systems, such as a returns query, can be inaccurate.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 181–182"},{id:"sv-2-1339",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`A linearity study shows that a gauge's bias changes across its range. Put the follow-up steps in order:

- **P** Recalibrate the gauge across the range
- **Q** Repeat the linearity study to confirm the fix
- **R** Have the gauge maintained or repaired
- **S** Release the gauge for baseline data collection`,options:["P → R → Q → S","R → P → Q → S","R → Q → P → S","R → P → S → Q"],answer:1,hint:"Fix the instrument, set it, prove it, then use it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 183: linearity problems often indicate a need to **replace or perform maintenance** on the equipment; calibration then controls bias (p. 182)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Repair → recalibrate across the range → re-run the linearity study → release for use."}],final:"Repair → recalibrate → confirm → release → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — maintenance changes the gauge, so calibrating before it would be wasted.","","Incorrect — the confirming study should follow recalibration, not precede it.","Incorrect — the gauge must not be released before the fix is confirmed."],bookRef:"Book pp. 182–183"},{id:"sv-2-1340",phase:2,topic:"2.3.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Two laboratories report different results for the **same batch samples**. Put the steps to resolve the dispute in order:

- **P** Calibrate the instrument found to be biased
- **Q** Identify which instrument is biased
- **R** Re-test the disputed samples
- **S** Test the same certified reference sample in both labs`,options:["Q → S → P → R","S → P → Q → R","S → Q → P → R","S → Q → R → P"],answer:2,hint:"Reference first, diagnose, correct, then re-measure.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 181: accuracy is assessed by measuring a **known standard**; p. 182: calibration controls bias."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Reference sample in both labs → find the biased instrument → calibrate it → re-test the disputed samples."}],final:"Reference → diagnose → calibrate → re-test → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a biased instrument cannot be identified before both labs have tested the reference sample.","Incorrect — calibrating before knowing which instrument is biased may adjust the wrong one.","","Incorrect — re-testing before calibrating repeats the original error."],bookRef:"Book pp. 176, 181–182"}];export{e as items};
